{"step_index":144,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-15T20:29:49Z","content":"Priyanshu,\n\n### ANSWER\nI executed the `/find-skills` command against the global agent skills registry, searching specifically for video automation, animation, and YouTube pipelines. \n\nIf you want the maximum-installed agent skills that can execute directly in your terminal, here are the absolute top-tier tools in the world right now:\n\n### WHY\nThe `find-skills` registry ranks by installation count. Here is what the top creators and developers are actually installing to automate their terminal pipelines:\n\n**1. The Native Code Pipeline (Highest Recommended)**\n*   **`remotion-dev/skills@remotion-best-practices` (525,000+ installs)**\n*   **`remotion-dev/skills@remotion-create` (82,900+ installs)**\n*   **`remotion-dev/skills@remotion-captions` (80,700+ installs)**\n*   *What it does:* This is the official toolkit for Remotion. If you install these skills, your AI terminal agent gains the ability to write, debug, and render programmatic video pipelines locally on your machine, complete with automated subtitles.\n\n**2. The AI Generation & Animation Pipelines**\n*   **`genmedia-labs/skills@ai-video-generation` (502,000+ installs)**\n*   **`heygen-com/hyperframes@hyperframes-animation` (425,000+ installs)**\n*   **`prime-skills/runcomfy-agent-skills@video-edit` (420,000+ installs)**\n*   *What it does:* These skills allow your terminal agent to orchestrate complex AI video generation (image-to-video, deepfake avatars, and ComfyUI node workflows) directly from the command line.\n\n**3. YouTube-Specific Workflow Skills**\n*   **`higgsfield-ai/skills@higgsfield-youtube-thumbnail` (53,700+ installs)**\n*   **`jimliu/baoyu-skills@baoyu-youtube-transcript` (20,400+ installs)**\n*   **`claude-office-skills/skills@youtube-automation` (4,400+ installs)**\n*   *What it does:* Specialized skills to scrape YouTube transcripts for research, generate high-CTR thumbnails, and automate channel management tasks.\n\n### COUNTER\nYou asked for \"better alternatives\" and assumed that finding skills with \"maximum stars\" would give you free, open-source magic bullets. \n\nThe brutal reality of the `skills.sh` registry is that the highest-installed video skills (GenMedia, HeyGen, Higgsfield) are **API wrappers for expensive commercial companies**. They are not free, open-source alternatives to VideoScribe. They exist solely to let AI coding agents easily send rendering requests to their paid cloud servers. If you install them, you still have to pay for the API credits.\n\n### CONDITIONS\nIf your goal is to build a high-quality faceless channel while keeping your software costs at zero, **ignore the AI generation wrappers**. \n\nInstead, open your terminal and install the official Remotion skills:\n`powershell -Command \"npx skills add remotion-dev/skills@remotion-create -g -y\"`\n`powershell -Command \"npx skills add remotion-dev/skills@remotion-captions -g -y\"`\n\nBy giving your AI coding agent these skills, it can write the raw React code to generate your whiteboard graphics and auto-captions locally on your own hardware, entirely for free.\n\n### SOURCES\n*   `npx skills find` live registry data (Video, Animation, Remotion, and YouTube queries)","thinking":"**Analyzing Skill Distribution**\n\nI've finished going through all the sub-tasks. My focus is now on synthesizing the findings. I'm seeing a clear pattern emerging from the `npx skills` registry. While many video generation skills exist, they fall into specific categories. One key observation: GenMedia Labs (`ai-video-generation`) seems prominent, and I want to investigate further.\n\n\n"}
